# Probability: Measuring Uncertainty

> Probability puts a number between 0 and 1 on how likely something is:
> 0 = impossible, 1 = certain. Everything else lives in between.

## 1. The Basic Definition

[definition]
For equally likely outcomes:

@math label="eq:probability-def"
P(E) = \frac{\text{number of favourable outcomes}}{\text{total number of outcomes}}
@/math
[/definition]

## Example

One card drawn from a standard 52-card deck. $P(\text{king}) =
\frac{4}{52} = \frac{1}{13}$ — four kings, fifty-two cards.

## 2. Complements: The "Not" Shortcut

@math label="eq:complement"
P(\text{not } E) = 1 - P(E)
@/math

The chance of **at least one** six in three dice rolls is easier from
the other side: $1 - \left(\frac{5}{6}\right)^3 = 1 - \frac{125}{216}
= \frac{91}{216}$.

## 3. Combining Events

| Situation | Rule |
| --- | --- |
| A and B are independent | $P(A \text{ and } B) = P(A) \cdot P(B)$ |
| A and B cannot both happen (mutually exclusive) | $P(A \text{ or } B) = P(A) + P(B)$ |
| General "or" | $P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cap B)$ |

## Example

Two coins flipped. $P(\text{two heads}) = \frac12 \times \frac12 =
\frac14$ — independent events multiply. But $P(\text{head or tail on
one coin}) = \frac12 + \frac12 = 1$ — mutually exclusive events add.

## 4. Sample Spaces You Should Know Cold

| Experiment | Outcomes | Example probability |
| --- | --- | --- |
| One die | 6 | P(prime) = 3/6 = 1/2 (2, 3, 5) |
| Two dice | 36 | P(sum = 7) = 6/36 = 1/6 |
| Two coins | 4 | P(exactly one head) = 2/4 = 1/2 |

The sum-7 result deserves a picture — six of the thirty-six ordered
pairs make it the most likely total:

