## 5. Experimental vs Theoretical

Flip a fair coin 10 times and you might see 7 heads — experimental
probability wobbles at small samples. As trials grow, it converges to
the theoretical $\frac12$: that convergence is the **law of large
numbers**.

[important]
Probabilities of ALL possible outcomes always sum to exactly 1. If your
list sums to more, you double-counted; to less, you missed a case.
[/important]

## Key Takeaways

- Favourable ÷ total for equally likely cases; complement flips the question.
- Multiply independent events; add mutually exclusive ones.
- The two-dice distribution peaks at 7 — and every list must total 1.
